Subject: [PATCH 11151/11171] [outer-loop] Record three tests un-collectable
 for five days, hidden by sharding

pytest tests/python --collect-only reports 61,773 collected and 3 errors. Three
test files import carnot experiment modules that do not exist: no
python/carnot module, no scripts/experiments script, and no results artifact,
so the experiments were never implemented. A sibling test imports the same way
and works, so the shape is right and the modules are simply absent. git log
--all finds zero commits for any of them; the tests landed 2026-08-31.

Nobody saw it because the conductor's per-task test phase runs a narrow shard,
93 to 121 tests, and a shard that excludes a broken file cannot report it. A
green test phase is not evidence the suite collects. Sharded verification is
blind to whole-suite collection errors by construction.

Also records a measurement I got wrong while finding it. I first counted test
files whose python/carnot module is absent and got 1776 of 3902, which reads
like a catastrophe and is an artifact: most never import a module of that name,
so the count measured a path convention rather than breakage. The honest number
is the 3 pytest already reported.

Not fixed: the repair is to write the modules or delete the tests, and deleting
conductor-authored tests is destructive and not mine to choose.

One test-suite-mutation marker deleted deliberately. It was armed by my own
read-only --collect-only run and named exactly one path, ops/conductor-log.md,
marked [NOT this run] -- the conductor appending its own log inside my
collection window. I never write that file. Reverting it would have destroyed
conductor entries, which is the loss the guard's own CAUTION describes.

